---
// Lesson 1.2 — The Unity Editor Tour
// The five panes, GameObject/Component/Transform, the real anatomy of the
// Player (Transform + an EMPTY script + a child "Sprite" carrying the
// SpriteRenderer and Animator), and play mode vs edit mode.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.2 — The Unity Editor Tour</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Before you write a line of code you need to read the tool that runs everything.
    Unity is five windows, one Inspector, and a trap called play mode. Ten minutes
    of tour saves ten hours of confusion — and along the way you will discover
    something strange about the Player in this project.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tour</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Open the Overworld scene. Find and name all five panes:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Pane</th><th>What it shows</th><th>Your move</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Hierarchy</strong> (left)</td><td>Every GameObject in this scene, as a tree</td><td>Click things. Some are parents with children — why?</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Scene</strong> (centre)</td><td>The world, editable from above</td><td>Scroll to zoom, right-drag to pan, F to focus</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Game</strong></td><td>What the camera sees when you press Play</td><td>Switch back and forth with the tabs</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Inspector</strong> (right)</td><td>Everything about the selected object — as components</td><td>Select the Player and read the whole list</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Project</strong> (bottom)</td><td>The repo's actual files: Scenes, Scripts, Prefabs, Artwork</td><td>Navigate to Assets/Scripts and find PlayerController.cs</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        <strong>The one idea to remember: a GameObject is an empty container, and
        components are the abilities you bolt onto it.</strong> Select the Player in
        the Hierarchy and expand its children. This is everything it has:
    </p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Object</th><th>Component</th><th>What it does here</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td rowspan="3"><strong>Player</strong><br><small>(tagged <code>Player</code>)</small></td>
            <td><strong>Transform</strong></td>
            <td>Position, rotation, and scale — every GameObject has one</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Player Controller (Script)</strong></td>
            <td><strong>Nothing yet.</strong> Select it and count the fields: there are none. It is an empty shell waiting for you.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><em>(no Sprite Renderer here)</em></td>
            <td>The Player itself is invisible. Keep reading.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td rowspan="3"><strong>Sprite</strong><br><small>(a child of Player)</small></td>
            <td><strong>Transform</strong></td>
            <td>Position <em>relative to its parent</em> — this is why children move with their parent</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Sprite Renderer</strong></td>
            <td><strong>What you actually see</strong> on screen — the picture of your character</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Animator</strong></td>
            <td>The idle animation that plays when you press Play</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Why is the picture on a child object?</strong> This is a very common
        Unity pattern. The parent <em>Player</em> is the thing you move, collide, and
        track. The child <em>Sprite</em> is just the artwork hanging off it. Keep this
        split in mind — in a few lessons, finding components on children rather than
        on the root will matter a great deal.
    </div>

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Trap</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Play mode is a dream, edit mode is reality.</strong> Press Play.
        While the game runs, drag a Coin prefab from <code>Assets/Prefabs</code>
        into the scene. Stop the game. The coin is <em>gone</em>. Everything you change
        in play mode — positions, Inspector values, even new objects — evaporates when
        you press stop. Students lose whole afternoons to this every year. You will
        lose five minutes once, and never again.
    </div>

    <p><strong>See it happen:</strong> press Play, select the Player, and change
    <strong>Transform Position X</strong> to <code>15</code> in the Inspector.
    Your character teleports across the screen. Press stop. Where is the Player now,
    and where did your change go?</p>

    <p>
        The safe version of that workflow: change values in play mode to
        <em>experiment and test</em>, then stop and enter the winning numbers in edit
        mode so they persist. For the rest of the year this is your primary live-tuning
        workflow — you will use it in the very next lesson to find a movement speed
        that feels right.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why would Unity be designed so that play-mode changes vanish? Think about testing an idea fifty times a day — what does "reset to the last known good game" buy you?</li>
            <li>Unticking the Player Controller changed nothing at all. What does that tell you about the relationship between a component and the code inside it?</li>
            <li>Is play mode closer to a save-file system or to an undo button, and why?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Ship It</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        One more piece of housekeeping: scenes are files like anything else. When you
        move something in the Overworld, the <code>.unity</code> file is dirty — Ctrl+S
        saves the scene (look for the asterisk in the tab). Save, then commit and push:
        <code>"1.2: editor tour"</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Name all five panes and what each one is for</li>
            <li>Explain "GameObject = container, components = abilities" using the Player as the example</li>
            <li>Point at which object carries the Sprite Renderer and which carries the script</li>
            <li>Demonstrate play-mode vanishing with your own two hands</li>
            <li>Save a scene and commit + push the change</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — the editor, scripts, input, vectors</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — every API this course uses</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="1.3.html">1.3 — Your First Script: Variables &amp; Update</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="1.2" title="1.2 The Unity Editor Tour — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
